1
00:00:00,641 --> 00:00:02,273
One hunter, or a colony?

2
00:00:03,065 --> 00:00:05,098
Trace that pale line through the water.

3
00:00:05,929 --> 00:00:08,061
Where would you say one animal ends and

4
00:00:08,073 --> 00:00:09,124
the next begins?

5
00:00:10,645 --> 00:00:13,657
Look at this photograph from a dive off Puerto Rico.

6
00:00:14,507 --> 00:00:15,498
Start at the tip.

7
00:00:16,288 --> 00:00:18,820
Follow the row of clear bells down to the bend.

8
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:22,031
See how the pattern changes there?

9
00:00:22,902 --> 00:00:24,933
If these were just links in a chain,

10
00:00:25,203 --> 00:00:27,735
you'd expect the next link to do the same thing.

11
00:00:29,126 --> 00:00:30,337
Here's the surprise.

12
00:00:31,189 --> 00:00:33,422
A siphonophore grows as a colony.

13
00:00:34,253 --> 00:00:36,927
The repeating parts are animals called zooids,

14
00:00:37,157 --> 00:00:38,509
and they stay attached.

15
00:00:39,770 --> 00:00:41,401
Watch a swimming bell squeeze.

16
00:00:42,252 --> 00:00:44,022
Water shoots out of the opening.

17
00:00:44,813 --> 00:00:47,202
The push moves the bell and carries its

18
00:00:47,214 --> 00:00:48,545
neighbors along with it.

19
00:00:49,816 --> 00:00:53,210
Farther down the stem, a tentacle trails in the water.

20
00:00:54,081 --> 00:00:55,994
Its stinging cells can catch prey.

21
00:00:56,845 --> 00:00:59,819
A feeding zooid has the mouth that takes the meal in.

22
00:01:01,151 --> 00:01:02,803
So where does one hunter end?

23
00:01:03,675 --> 00:01:06,569
You can pick out the individual swimmers and feeders.

24
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:09,755
But follow a meal or a single stroke,

25
00:01:10,005 --> 00:01:12,319
and you're watching the whole colony at work.

